   "description": "The MX2010 as the vendor photographed its front, as far as the photograph identifies the cards. LC7 and LC8 are UNCERTAIN at about 1 px/mm: LC7 may be an MPC-3D-16XGE-SFPP rather than an MPC2E, and LC8's lower MIC a 20GE SFP MIC; they are drawn as an MPC2E with blank MIC windows and a blank lower window rather than as a guess.",
   "kind": "example",
   "source": "photo - the Juniper image-library MX2010 front (#261): every lower slot holds an adapter and an MX240-form MPC. LC0 an MPC with two 4-port XFP MICs; LC2 to LC5 the 32x10GE MPC4E (dense SFP+ columns); LC6 a 4-port XFP MIC over a 2-port one; LC8 a 2-port XFP MIC over an 8-SFP MIC; LC9 two 2-port XFP MICs. The carrier of the XFP MICs is drawn as the MPC2E, the base carrier that takes both. LC1 holds a dual-wide 40-port RJ45 MIC, which no bay can seat yet, and LC7 and LC8's 8-SFP MICs are not identifiable at about 1 px/mm, so those windows carry blanks."
  }
 },
 "datasheet": {
  "sha256": "ebc1a81a0210f826571936a4bfa6d41a98de5bc446aabc8d081a51c169a8e494",
  "title": "Juniper MX2000 Series datasheet"
 },
 "description": "Juniper MX2010 - the 34RU half-height member of the MX2000 family. Craft interface and EMI cover at the top; an upper cage with a CB-RE at each end and eight Switch Fabric Boards between them; a lower cage of ten vertical MPC slots (LC 0-9, each seated through an adapter card); and at the rear a nine-module PSM wall between the two power distribution modules, with fan trays behind the grilles at both ends.",
 "format": 1,
 "gaps": [
  {
   "reason": "needs-photo",
   "scope": [
    "fpcs"
   ],
   "wanted": "the MICs in the photographed unit's LC7 and LC8 (eight SFP cages each) are not identifiable at about 1 px/mm, and LC1's dual-wide 40-port RJ45 MIC cannot be seated until a module can fill two bays. Since roc-ops/Portrayal#261 the MX240-form MPCs seat here through the adapter card (juniper/mx2000-lc-adapter@1), so the range is the MX960's.",
   "what": "mpc-catalogue"
  },
  {
   "reason": "needs-photo",
   "scope": [
    "res"
   ],
   "wanted": "a close photograph of the CB-RE faceplate - the jack stack is summarised from a ~1 px/mm image and the per-jack legends are unreadable. At 3.0.0 the CB-RE took the guides' 43.18 x 412.1 (#902) and its jack column moved 5.9 mm left to stay centred; the photograph shows CB-RE 0's stack left of its plate's centre and CB-RE 1's right of it, which a close photograph would settle.",
   "what": "cb-re-face-detail"
  },
  {
   "reason": "needs-photo",
   "wanted": "the four fan trays and two PDMs as FRU bays - they sit behind the rear grilles and zones drawn as decor; tray/PDM face photographs would let them become bays.",
   "what": "fan-trays-and-pdms"
  },
  {
   "reason": "needs-photo",
   "scope": [
    "psus"
   ],
   "wanted": "the AC, 240V-China and HVAC/HVDC PSM faces; the photographed unit is -48 V DC.",
   "what": "psu-variants"
  },
  {
   "note": "The guide states a figure and it does not hold together, so none is carried. Table 60 gives the base system (no MPCs, ADCs or MICs) 9,439 W at 55 C and 2,142 W typical, identical to the MX2020 base row (MX2020 guide Table 65) for a different base system: this row lists two fan trays and four PSMs, the MX2020 row four and eight. 2,142 W is also exactly Table 71's typical INPUT power per PSM. The row's two fan trays disagree with Table 61, which lists four (two upper, two lower). And the DC thermal example (Table 84) assumes 34.5 kW of output for a loaded chassis, more than the 22.5 kW Table 82 gives the whole system, and its input line divides 16.5 by 0.91 to reach 37.9 kW; the MX2020 guide uses the same 34.5 kW. Table 80's sample counts 5.5 kW for the CB-REs, SFBs and fan trays alone.",
   "reason": "sources-disagree",
   "scope": [
    "fact:peak-power-w",
    "fact:typical-power-w"
   ],
   "wanted": "an MX2010 power-planning page or datasheet that states a chassis draw for this chassis rather than one shared with the MX2020.",
   "what": "chassis-power-figure"
  }
 ],
